-- title:
--   Maximal ideal at a hyperbola point generated by v-y'
-- statement:
--   Let $W$ be a commutative ring which is a domain, and let $t,x',y'\in W$ satisfy $x'y'=t$ and $y'\neq 0$. Put $Q=W[X_0,X_1]/(X_0X_1-t)$, the quotient of `MvPolynomial (Fin 2) W` by the ideal generated by $X_0X_1-C(t)$, let $M=\operatorname{Spec}Q$ as a scheme, and let $\varphi\colon Q\to\Gamma(M,\top)$ be the ring homomorphism underlying the inverse of the canonical isomorphism between $Q$ and the global sections of $\operatorname{Spec}Q$. Let $L\colon Q\to W$ be the ring homomorphism underlying the $W$-algebra map `CrossingQuotient.lift` attached to the pair $(x',y')$, i.e. the map induced by evaluating $X_0\mapsto x'$, $X_1\mapsto y'$, which is well defined because $x'y'=t$. Assume that $\ker L$ is a prime ideal, and let $m\in M$ be the corresponding point of $\operatorname{Spec}Q$. Then the maximal ideal of the stalk $\mathcal O_{M,m}$ is the principal ideal generated by the germ at $m$ of the global section $\varphi(\,$`CrossingQuotient.V t`$\,-\,y'\,)$, where `CrossingQuotient.V t` is the distinguished element of $Q$ attached to the second coordinate.
--
--   This is the local statement that at a $W$-point $(x',y')$ with $y'\neq0$ of the affine hyperbola $\operatorname{Spec}W[u,v]/(uv-t)$ — the standard chart of an ordinary double point — the function $v-y'$ is a uniformiser of the local ring. It is used in the analysis of a semistable model of a modular curve, where such a chart supplies the local parameter computed in [`ModularCurve.XHDRModelAtP.ord_read_chart_sub_algebraMap_eq_one_of_section_of_chart`](thm.html#ModularCurve.XHDRModelAtP.ord_read_chart_sub_algebraMap_eq_one_of_section_of_chart).
